session:pr349Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "docs/plans/RML_SQL_PLAN.md already designed a two-tier SQL join-pushdown strategy, but its own opening note explicitly gated that design on separate user sign-off before any code was written -- that sign-off hadn't happened, so this PR implements only phases 1-2 (SqlSource reading SQLite via rusqlite, rml:tableName whole-table scans and rml:sqlQuery arbitrary SELECTs) and deliberately adds no JoinAlgorithm::SqlPushdown variant or choose_join_algorithm function, since nothing would execute them yet and an unused enum variant would misrepresent what's implemented. Instead, one end-to-end test proves the existing rml:joinCondition hash-join engine (RML_JOIN_PLAN.md) already composes with the new SQL source for free -- scan_rows is source-agnostic, so a SQL child joined against a SQL parent on the same SQLite file produces correct triples through the ordinary hash join, just without a synthesized pushdown query. The plan doc's pinned rusqlite version (0.31) was stale; cargo add resolved 0.40.1 instead. Three implementation details the plan didn't fully specify: SQLite's dynamically-typed columns are converted to RawRow's String values via an explicit ValueRef match (BLOB rejected, since RML has no binary term type); NULL columns are omitted from the row entirely rather than stored as empty strings, matching CsvRow::get_str's existing null/absent-is-equivalent convention; and table names (which can't be bound as query parameters) are identifier-quoted with doubled embedded quotes before interpolation into 'SELECT * FROM <name>', tested against an actual quote-containing table name to rule out SQL injection via the identifier position. Also added a read-only OpenFlags connection and ran the confined (sandbox.rs) path through both validate_mapping_sources and scan_rows, consistent with how CSV/JSON/XML sources are already sandboxed." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:349 ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr349 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .
